From: ios-milanf-port (desk phase, 2026-10-25)
Subject: [PATCH] MILANF: the /dev/null tap, which is a daemon's last words

`launchd` starts every daemon with stdout and stderr on `/dev/null`. That is
fine on a machine with a terminal and fatal on this one: a process that prints
its reason for dying into `/dev/null` has printed it into nothing, and the next
thing you see is that it is gone. Pointing a launchd job at `/dev/console`
instead does not work - the tree records the observation, on the v8 image, that
it stops `backboardd` from launching at all.

So the kernel taps the null device. Minor 2 of `/dev/null` is the write path, and
`mmrw()` checks the writing process against a name list before discarding the
data:

```c
#if defined(ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_SWEET)
{
        char name[32];
        if (sweet_null_tee(name, sizeof(name))) {
                char buf[256];
                c = MIN(uio_curriovlen(uio), sizeof(buf));
                error = uiomove(buf, (int)c, uio);
                if (error) { break; }
                printf("|%s| %.*s%s", name, (int)c, buf,
                    (c > 0 && buf[c - 1] == '\n') ? "" : "\n");
                continue;
        }
}
#endif
```

Two details in that shape are load-bearing and easy to break:

*The `continue` is what makes it safe.* A match *consumes* the write itself -
`uiomove` into the buffer, print, `continue` - and never reaches the fall-through
`c = uio_curriovlen(uio); error = 0;` that the null device normally uses to
discard everything. Nothing is consumed twice and nothing is printed twice.

*Non-matching processes are untouched.* `sweet_null_tee` name-matches and returns
false, control falls into the original path, and the write is discarded exactly as
before. The cost for every other process is one `proc_selfname` and a walk over a
comma-separated list - the same shape as 0096's filter, on a much colder path.

This is the third of the three transcripts, and the three are deliberately
distinct:

| patch | answers |
| --- | --- |
| 0096 `sweetiolog` | which IOKit service did a **live** daemon ask for before it gave up |
| 0098 `sweet_crash_log` | **where** was a process when it died |
| 0100 `sweetnull` | **what did** a process print before it died |

On a device with no serial port and no way to get logs off it, that is the whole
toolkit, and this is the cheapest of the three: it needs no registry, no frames,
no symbolication, just the bytes the daemon itself chose to say.

On milanf it is arguably the most valuable of the three, because the board's
userspace brings up in stages and each stage fails by a daemon printing a reason
and exiting. `launchd`'s own log goes to the console, but a job that fails after
`exec` does not - unless it is in this list.

Usage:

```
tools/mkpayload.py Image ADT out.img \
    --cmdline "debug=0xc serial=3 cpus=1 keepsyms=1 \
               sweetnull=launchd,backboardd,SpringBoard"
```

The default list stays `backboardd,SpringBoard` exactly as sweet has it, and the
boot-arg name and the `|name|` prefix are deliberately unchanged: the host-side
log tooling greps them, and on this port those strings are the interface - same
rule as 0096.

`PE_parse_boot_argn`, `proc_selfname` and `printf` are all already compiled on
milanf; nothing in the block reads a sweet address, clock or register. The gate is
the whole difference.

Verified without a compiler: fresh ci-tail (ci-art3 + 0086..0099), both gates
asserted at exactly 2 occurrences before replacing, header comment reworded
board-neutrally, patch CR=0, source CR=0, 0086-0100 sequential apply clean. Link
unverified; CI in the morning.

diff --git a/bsd/dev/mem.c b/bsd/dev/mem.c
index 291a546..b3eacdd 100644
--- a/bsd/dev/mem.c
+++ b/bsd/dev/mem.c
@@ -153,14 +153,16 @@ mmioctl(dev_t dev, u_long cmd, __unused caddr_t data,
 	return 0;
 }
 
-#if defined(ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_SWEET)
+#if defined(ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_SWEET) || defined(ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_MILANF)
 /*
- * SWEET: daemons started by launchd write stdout/stderr to /dev/null, and
- * pointing a launchd job at /dev/console stops it from launching at all
- * (backboardd, v8 image). Instead, what the processes named in boot-arg
- * sweetnull (comma separated, default "backboardd,SpringBoard") write to
- * /dev/null goes to the console (and so to the persistent log) as
- * "|name| text". sweetnull=- turns it off.
+ * /dev/null tap for the Qualcomm boards: daemons started by launchd
+ * write stdout/stderr to /dev/null, and pointing a launchd job at
+ * /dev/console stops it from launching at all (backboardd, v8 image).
+ * Instead, what the processes named in boot-arg sweetnull (comma
+ * separated, default "backboardd,SpringBoard") write to /dev/null goes
+ * to the console (and so to the persistent log) as "|name| text".
+ * sweetnull=- turns it off. The boot-arg name and the prefix are kept
+ * as-is: the host-side tooling greps them.
  */
 static char sweet_null_names[128];
 static int sweet_null_parsed;
@@ -254,7 +256,7 @@ mmrw(dev_t dev, struct uio *uio, enum uio_rw rw)
 			if (rw == UIO_READ) {
 				return 0;
 			}
-#if defined(ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_SWEET)
+#if defined(ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_SWEET) || defined(ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_MILANF)
 			{
 				char name[32];
 				if (sweet_null_tee(name, sizeof(name))) {
